# Re-architect the realism core to two-velocity-field PB-MPM (stiff grid, no Poisson) — requirements

## Summary

The coffee-pour water must **bounce/scatter on impact** and read as real water, while keeping the two assets the current two-field solver has — the **coffee/water coupling** and the **frame rate** (real-time, ~200k particles / ≤33 ms). A long patching campaign on the two-field grid solver (surface-weighted dissipation transfer, density-relief/density-target pressure, merge discriminator) did **not** deliver realistic water. The converged diagnosis: **a bounce is a stiff-incompressibility (pressure) response — the pool must spike pressure and shove the impacting water back** — and the two-field's **under-converged velocity-divergence (`∇·v`) Jacobi projection is fundamentally soft** (it's fast *because* it under-converges, which is the same reason it can't bounce). A fully-converged grid Poisson would bounce but is slow (`main`'s sparse-RBGS Poisson is ~70% of its frame).

**Decision: re-architect the realism core to two-velocity-field PB-MPM** (Position-Based MPM — the SOTA fan-out's candidate **A**). PB-MPM keeps everything **on the grid** (the proven perf path here: two-field runs 25 ms@207k vs the XPBD particle core's ~90 ms@200k) but replaces the soft `∇·v` projection with a **compliant, position-based density constraint** (the XPBD/PBF mechanism) that is **stable at any stiffness with a few iterations → it bounces — and uses no global Poisson → it stays fast**. XPBD is the in-repo existence proof that few-iteration compliant position constraints bounce in real time. The coffee/water **coupling is rebuilt** on this substrate (the accepted, publishable core of the work): two co-located momentum fields, the constraint multiplier `λ` as the pore-pressure field, pore-packing density target, Laibe-Price drag, XPBI bed plasticity, and conserved absorption — patterns proven in pieces across XPBD and the two-field, but never assembled as a two-velocity *porous* PB-MPM (EA SEED's shipped PB-MPM is single-phase water).

This supersedes the two-field-patching path; the two-field, `main` MPM, and XPBD become reference/referee solvers (and donors of portable pieces + the validation harness).

## Problem Frame

- **Bounce = stiff incompressibility.** Water bounces/scatters off the cup/pool only if the fluid is stiff enough that an impact spikes pressure and reflects the incoming momentum. XPBD and `main` MPM bounce; two-field does not — it "just pools where it is" (visual oracle). Empirically, every two-field water failure (mushy/no-bounce, over-pack, over-buoyant coffee, settled stirring) traces to its `∇·v` projection: it's under-converged (residual compressibility), it doesn't even constrain density, and the projection dissipates the impact momentum that should bounce.
- **The perf↔stiffness trap on the classical grid.** Converge the Poisson → stiff but slow; under-converge → fast but soft. Two-field chose fast/soft. `main` chose a converged-ish RBGS Poisson (~70% of frame, with an operator-inconsistency divergence floor). Neither gives bounce **and** speed.
- **PB-MPM escapes the trap.** It has **no global Poisson**. Incompressibility is a **compliant position-based density constraint** (XPBD-style): the compliance absorbs stiffness so a few iterations give a stiff *restoring force* on density error (→ push-back → bounce) without converging anything. Same iteration budget as two-field's loop; opposite stiffness behavior. EA SEED ships this real-time in WebGPU.
- **The grid is the perf path here.** Measured in-repo: two-field grid = 25.4 ms@207k; XPBD particle core = ~90 ms@200k (linear after the reorder, levers unpulled). Staying on the grid (PB-MPM) keeps the frame-rate asset; a particle core would have to claw back ~3.6×.
- **The coupling must survive the move.** Coffee/water coupling (infiltration/saturation, buoyancy, crater, Terzaghi, conserved absorption) is an asset and is conservation-critical. It is the part PB-MPM must **rebuild** (the novel contribution), because PB-MPM's incompressibility is position-based, not the cell-centered `∇p` the two-field coupling consumes.

## Key Decisions

- **KD1 — Position-based compliant density constraint replaces the `∇·v` projection.** This is the bounce-and-speed reconciliation: stiff at any stiffness with a few P2G→solve→G2P iterations (compliance absorbs stiffness), no global Poisson. The constraint targets **density** (J / volume), so it cannot suffer the two-field's density-blind over-pack. (Existence proof: XPBD/PBF bounces real-time today with this mechanism.)
- **KD2 — Stay fully on the grid; port EA SEED's WebGPU PB-MPM as the single-phase water reference.** Honors the frame-rate asset; gives a concrete reference for the water core. PB-MPM's 3D incompressibility convergence needs a **coarse-grid pressure pre-pass** (a small multigrid-ish helper, *not* a full Poisson) — the one "unbuilt piece" to build.
- **KD3 — Two co-located momentum fields (water + solid) for percolation.** Water and grains move at different velocities in the same cell. Laibe-Price analytic exponential drag (exact at any stiffness, O(1)/pair) folds into the constraint iteration.
- **KD4 — Mixture incompressibility: water density target `ρ₀·(1−φ_s)` (pore-packing).** Per-cell volume fractions `φ_s` (solid) and pore fraction `n = 1−φ_s`; saturation `s = φ_f/n`. Water packs into the pore space rather than expelling grains. (Proven in XPBD's coupling — `targets ρ₀·(1−α_s)`.)
- **KD5 — `λ` (constraint multiplier) IS the pore-pressure field.** Buoyancy and the Terzaghi effective-stress partition derive from `∇λ` — exactly as XPBD already derives buoyancy from its PBF `λ`. The over-buoyant-coffee issue should resolve here as physical buoyancy, not a tune.
- **KD6 — Conserved absorption/wetting, ported.** Water↔grain transfer with `water_lost == grain_gained` exactly + effective mass for momentum (swelling), from XPBD `wetting.wgsl` / two-field U9. Conservation is gate-enforced (volume + total-water + the long-run saturated-tail regime).
- **KD7 — XPBI for the bed plasticity/crater.** Klar et al. Drucker-Prager return map run inside the position-based loop (XPBI, arXiv 2405.11694) — pressure-dependent yield, tamping memory, the crater that holds. Plus a small APIC affine momentum on the pour/free-water to sustain the cavity (the one thing pure position-projection dissipates).
- **KD8 — Perf is proven FIRST.** The very first build is a minimal prototype (EA SEED single-phase water PB-MPM) that measures **both the bounce and the frame rate at ~200k** before any coupling is built. The whole effort is gated on that prototype clearing real-time; the coupling rebuild and the perf are the two risks retired by measurement, not assumption.
- **KD9 — Build along the validation ladder.** Isolate each interaction, then pairwise, then all three (per the SOTA program): water-only (bounce + perf) → + bed plasticity (crater/repose) → + two-field percolation/drag → + saturation/absorption → full coupled pour-over. Each rung gets its own gates; the existing render harness + Debug Scenes catalog + the twofield/xpbd test families are the validation substrate.

## Requirements / Success Criteria

- **R1 — Water bounces/scatters on impact** (crown/rebound off the cup floor, walls, and standing pool), lively free surface, not "pools where it is." Primary oracle: the live webapp on the comparison scenes; quantitatively, a mass-weighted post-impact rebound/spread signal vs the two-field baseline.
- **R2 — Real-time:** ≤ 33 ms @ ~200k on the fixed-point i32 grid (no float atomics), under Tint uniformity, within the storage-buffer ceiling. **Verified by the KD8 prototype before the full build.**
- **R3 — Coffee/water coupling at two-field fidelity, conserved:** infiltration/drawdown at plausible rates, saturation, buoyancy (grains seat under a standing column — no over-buoyancy), a crater that holds (wet-sand plasticity), Terzaghi/Skempton effective-stress partition. Mass/volume conserved (free + absorbed water), gate-enforced incl. the long-run saturated tail.
- **R4 — Incompressibility is stiff and density-targeting:** no over-pack/dense-ring, no settled-pool stirring, the pool reads incompressible (holds shape, reflects impacts).
- **R5 — The architecture is a coherent whole** (one solver, the three interactions coupled), not a patchwork; the two-field/main/xpbd remain as reference/referee solvers and as the source of portable pieces (XPBI, conserved wetting, Laibe-Price drag, the render harness).

## Candidate Approaches

- **A — Two-velocity PB-MPM (chosen).** Grid, position-based compliant density constraint (stiff/bounce, no Poisson, fast), two momentum fields + `λ`-pore-pressure + Laibe-Price drag + XPBI bed + conserved wetting. Pros: reconciles bounce + frame-rate + grid-coupling; SOTA's publishable core; EA SEED water reference. Cons/risks: the two-velocity *porous* mixture is a novel rebuild (no shipped reference); the coarse-grid convergence pre-pass is unbuilt; perf of the assembled system is unproven (KD8 retires it).
- **B — Particle (XPBD) core + grid-assisted coupling (rejected).** Proven bounce + the coupling is more preserved (XPBD already has λ-coupling), but XPBD is ~90 ms@200k today (the frame-rate asset at risk) and a particle↔grid handoff adds cost/complexity. Loses on the perf asset the user prioritized.
- **C — Keep patching the two-field `∇·v` projection (rejected).** Soft by construction (the bounce↔convergence trap); the patching campaign empirically failed.

## Scope Boundaries

**In scope:** a re-architected two-velocity PB-MPM realism core — the position-based density constraint (water incompressibility/bounce), the coarse-grid convergence pre-pass, two momentum fields + Laibe-Price drag, the pore-packing mixture (`ρ₀·(1−φ_s)`, saturation), `λ`-based buoyancy/Terzaghi, conserved wetting/absorption, XPBI bed plasticity/crater, APIC-for-cavity; the KD8 perf prototype; the validation ladder gates.

### Deferred to Follow-Up / Build-Order
- Full coupled pour-over fidelity is the *last* rung; early rungs isolate water-only, then bed, then percolation, then absorption.
- Surface tension / sub-grid droplets (not required for bounce/liveliness).

**Outside this effort's identity:** the two-field / `main` / XPBD solvers are **not** modified to become the primary — they stay as reference/referee + donors of portable pieces; the just-built transfer work (U1–U3, uncommitted on `feat/twofield-surface-weighted-dissipation`) is superseded (kept as learnings). No global Poisson is introduced (the whole point is to avoid it).

## Outstanding Questions (for planning)

- **The KD8 perf prototype is the gating first step** — exact scene/particle count, the bounce metric, and the pass bar (≤33 ms@200k). If it fails, the whole architecture is reconsidered (fallback: candidate B, or accept two-field for coupling-only).
- **The coarse-grid pressure pre-pass** for PB-MPM 3D convergence: structure, iteration count, cost.
- **The conservation handshake** in the two-velocity mixture (P2G/G2P adjointness, volume/momentum conservation through the constraint loop + the wetting transfer) — port the two-field/XPBD conservation gates; watch the reorder stale-phase artifact.
- **Fixed-point i32 mapping** of the position-based constraint (`λ`, position deltas) on the no-float-atomics grid; FP overflow headroom; storage-buffer budget (the ≤16 ceiling) for two momentum fields + `λ` + bed state.
- **Migration shape:** a new solver in `src/solvers/` registered alongside the existing three (the registry pattern), reusing the scene/render/test harness.
- **What ports vs rebuilds:** XPBI (Klar return map), conserved wetting, Laibe-Price drag, the Debug Scenes + render harness are portable; the incompressibility core + two-velocity mixture + `λ`-coupling are new.

## Sources & Research

- **In-repo empirical record** (this session): two-field 25.4 ms@207k but soft/no-bounce; XPBD ~90 ms@200k but bounces (the existence proof) and already has λ-coupling + conserved wetting; `main` bounces via a slow RBGS Poisson + cup blow-up. `.deliberate/renders/` comparison; `docs/PERF_NOTES.md`; `docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md`.
- **PB-MPM** — Lewin, *A Position Based Material Point Method*, SIGGRAPH 2024 Talks; EA SEED WebGPU implementation (`electronicarts/pbmpm`, single-phase water reference).
- **XPBI** — Yu et al., SIGGRAPH Asia 2024 (Klar Drucker-Prager in a position-based loop) — the bed plasticity lift. **Klar et al. 2016** DP return map.
- **Laibe & Price 2012** — analytic exponential two-fluid drag (the stable Darcy coupling).
- **GIC (Granule-In-Cell), SIGGRAPH Asia 2025** — the closest particle+grid two-phase reference (validates the mixture physics + conservation; its non-real-time bottleneck is rigid-DEM substeps, which our continuum grains avoid).
- **`[[project_solver_redesign_sota]]`** — the prior 11-agent fan-out ranking these candidates (this decision = its candidate A); **`[[project_water_dynamics_unified]]`**, **`[[project_infiltration_ponding]]`**, **`[[project_twofield_cup_compressibility]]`**, **`[[project_perf_target]]`**.

