     1	// Wetting / absorption (XPBD step 4). Concatenated after common/water/bed/coupling.
     2	//
     3	// Dry grains absorb nearby water, conserving volume AND mass: the water volume transferred leaves
     4	// the fluid phase (water's remaining-volume fraction f_w shrinks) and becomes grain volume (V_abs
     5	// grows — the grain swells, applied in a later unit). Atomic-free and conservation-safe via a
     6	// deterministic, water-normalized two-sided allocation read from the frozen pred.w snapshot:
     7	//
     8	//   take_wg = min( f_w·V_w / N_w , demand_g / N_g )
     9	//
    10	// computed identically in the water pass (sums Σ_g take, shrinks its own f_w) and the grain pass
    11	// (sums Σ_w take into its own V_abs + the absorbed momentum). The two-sided cap bounds both per-water
    12	// (Σ_g ≤ f_w·V_w) and per-grain (Σ_w ≤ demand_g ≤ deficit, no V_cap overshoot). Each pass writes only
    13	// its own slot, so no atomics are needed. Runs after finalize; the next predict mirrors the new pos.w.
    14	//
    15	// moisture lane convention (pos.w / its frozen mirror pred.w): water = f_w ∈ [0,1], grain = V_abs ≥ 0.
    16	
    17	fn wet_v_cap() -> f32 { return params.r_max * params.rho_ratio * params.grain_volume; }
    18	// Saturation cutoff: a grain within `absorb_roundoff` of capacity is treated as full. Mirrors the
    19	// water lane's `absorb_roundoff` f_w deactivation floor. Without it the demand asymptotes toward
    20	// V_cap forever, and once the per-step `take` drops below the near-capacity grain's f32 ulp the grain
    21	// rounds its gain away (`v_abs + take == v_abs`) while the water still records the loss — a one-signed
    22	// volume sink that only shows up in the deep tail. Flooring demand to 0 here stops that tail cleanly.
    23	fn wet_sat_cutoff() -> f32 { let c = wet_v_cap(); return c - c * params.absorb_roundoff; }
    24	fn wet_v_water() -> f32 { return params.particle_mass / params.rest_density; }
    25	fn wet_demand(v_abs: f32) -> f32 {
    26	    if (v_abs >= wet_sat_cutoff()) { return 0.0; }
    27	    return (wet_v_cap() - v_abs) * (1.0 - exp(-params.k_abs * params.dt));
    28	}
